TWO DISCRIMINATING CELLS, ONE REAL AND ONE MINE.

glm's suite on gemini -- `unrecognised_status_word_is_unreadable_never_passed`:

    left: Passed { ran: 3 }   right: Unreadable

The superset section says an unrecognised shape is `Unreadable` and NEVER `Passed`, and says
why: `Unreadable` means "nothing established" and is the safe direction. gemini reads the counts
regardless of the status word and reports a pass. Real defect, against a clause that spells out
the failure mode it is preventing.

gemini's suite on or-nemotron -- `boundary_saturation_at_u32_max`:

    left: MatchedNothing { filtered_out: None }
    right: MatchedNothing { filtered_out: Some(4294967295) }

Mine. I pinned "counts larger than u32::MAX SATURATE" and, four bullets earlier, "a count that
does not parse as a number yields None". A count above u32::MAX does both. or-nemotron took the
second reading and gemini the first; neither is wrong. Vetoed and filed.

WHAT THE CRITICS FOUND.

gemini on glm is wholly favourable, and praises precisely the behaviour gemini itself gets
wrong: "Requiring `ok.` or `FAILED.` before count parsing correctly treats unrecognised status
tokens as `Unreadable` (the safe direction under the open-subset doctrine)."

glm on or-nemotron, three findings, all verified:

  "The status word drives the variant; counts only supply numbers. On 'ok. 0 passed; 1 failed'
   this returns `Passed { ran: 0 }` -- a value the spec declares nonexistent -- dropping the
   failed test, the 'everything fine' direction."

  and it declined to call that a defect: "Real cargo lines never disagree with their counts, so
  the spec underdetermines the fork and I record it as an ambiguity, not a defect (my own
  implementation went counts-driven); the task author should pin it." Disclosing that it took the
  other side of an unpinned fork is the discipline this stage exists to produce, and glm has now
  done it twice.

  "Two tests cannot fail: `verified_is_closed_set_of_four` asserts nothing." Checked -- it
  constructs five values, discards them, and has no assertion. or-nemotron has the most tests in
  the field and two of them cannot fail.

  Documentation, counted:  glm 30 //! + 99 ///,  gemini 31 + 37,  or-nemotron 0 + 18.
  or-nemotron has no module doc at all, as glm said.

DECISION. glm on survival -- the only arm passing every suite -- on a suite that found a real
defect in a rival, on documentation by a factor of three, and on the field's sharpest critique.
It has the fewest tests at 17; twenty-six including two that cannot fail is not depth.

1456 core tests, clippy 8.
